  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "louisiana-cajun-music-tunes-forms-and-style",
      "title": "Louisiana Cajun Music — Tunes, Forms, and Style",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** French & Acadian Folk | **Region:** french-louisiana-cajun | **Tier:** 4\n**Prerequisites:** regions-french-louisiana-cajun-03-instruments-voices-ii\n**Tags:** french, louisiana-cajun, french-acadian, tunes,, forms,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "two-step, waltz\n\nLouisiana Cajun Music belongs to the **French & Acadian Folk** curriculum. Dennis McGee\n\nThis lesson develops listening precision for **two-step, waltz** — connecting repertoire, instruments, dance, and the institutions that transmit music today.\n\n![Traditional musicians](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fiddle.jpg?width=640)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "compared-with-quebec",
      "title": "Compared with Quebec",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Quebecois Traditional Music** (`regions-french-quebec-04-genres-forms`) covers the same lesson slot from the anchor perspective. This lesson stresses: **two-step, waltz**.\n\n| Topic | Quebec anchor | Louisiana Cajun Music (this lesson) |\n|-------|------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Focus | reels, jigs, quadrilles… | two-step, waltz… |\n\n**Parallel anchor lesson:** `regions-french-quebec-04-genres-forms`\n**Secondary comparison (Ireland):** `regions-celtic-ireland-04-genres-forms`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "core-material",
      "title": "Core material",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "two-step, waltz\n\nStudy **louisiana cajun music** through listening first. Wikipedia's article on [Cajun music](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cajun_music) orients geography and history; recordings prove how musicians phrase ornaments, tempo, and ensemble balance in real rooms."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "regional-accent-and-social-context",
      "title": "Regional accent and social context",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Tradition lives in **community practice** — festivals, dances, teaching networks, and archives — not only on concert stages. For **French & Acadian Folk**, ask who leads (voice, fiddle, pipes, percussion), whether dance drives repertoire, and how revival institutions shaped what you hear on modern recordings."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "instruments-and-voices-in-this-lesson",
      "title": "Instruments and voices in this lesson",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Lead voices: **accordion, fiddle**. Ensemble layer: **guitar, triangle**. Compare timbre and role with the anchor unit before assuming techniques transfer unchanged."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "forms-dance-and-rhythm",
      "title": "Forms, dance, and rhythm",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Named forms include **two-step, waltz**. Dance context: **dance hall**. Metre and foot-tap feel are not decorative — they explain why musicians accent certain beats and phrase repeats."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "history-transmission-and-today",
      "title": "History, transmission, and today",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Historical threads: **Louisiana French communities**. Contemporary scenes: **Festivals Acadiens**. Tune repertoire anchor: **Jolie Blonde**. Follow lesson playlists and reading lists for recorded examples."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "listening-habits",
      "title": "Listening habits",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "1. Queue one field or archive recording and one contemporary ensemble track.\n2. Log metre, lead instrument, ornament, and social context in four columns.\n3. Return to the parallel anchor lesson if comparison feels abstract.\n4. Avoid generic world-music compilations when building ear training."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "study-facts",
      "title": "Study facts",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- two-step, waltz\n- Lead instruments: accordion, fiddle\n- Ensemble: guitar, triangle\n- Forms: two-step, waltz\n- Dance: dance hall\n- History: Louisiana French communities\n- Scenes: Festivals Acadiens\n- Repertoire: Jolie Blonde\n- Compared with Quebec: regional accent and social context differ.\n- Revival movements and media shaped twentieth-century practice.\n- Dance rhythm and tune metre are linked in this tradition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "key-points",
      "title": "Key points",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- Louisiana Cajun Music is a living tradition with regional accent.\n- Focus: two-step, waltz.\n- Compared with Quebec: shared heritage, distinct emphasis.\n- Forms: two-step, waltz.\n- Dance: dance hall.\n- Transmission: Louisiana French communities."
        }
      ]
    },
